      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i've been having a lot of trouble with my sourdough starter lately. the acidity levels seem to be spiking way too fast and it's making my dough really inconsistent. i'm trying to figure out if i should adjust the ambient temp in my kitchen or if i need to switch to a higher protein flour to stabilize the microbial activity. what's the best way to manage this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "An abrupt spike in acidity typically indicates that your starter is overfeeding or may require a more stable environment. Consider reducing the frequency of feedings and ensuring steady, cool temperatures, ideally between 70-75°F (21-24°C). Using warmer temperatures can also cause the yeast to over-thrive, increasing acidity. As for flour type, while adjusting flour protein can help with dough strength, it won’t necessarily solve the microbial activity imbalance. Maintaining a balanced feeding schedule and stable environment should first address your concern.",
