    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i've been keeping it around 78F which might be too warm. but even when i keep the temp steady, there's just this weird unpredictability to it. like, one day it's totally fine, and the next it's super sour and behaving almost aggressively, like it's actively fighting the baking process. is there a specific way the lactobacillus and yeast ratios shift during those sudden spikes that could explain why it feels so volatile?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "During the fermentation of sourdough, lactobacillus bacteria produce lactic acid, contributing to the sour flavor and the acidity of your starter. When the ratio of lactobacillus to yeast is imbalanced—often due to over-feeding or excessive fermentation—the lactobacillus growth rate can outpace that of the yeast, leading to sudden acidity spikes. This unrest can cause the observed \"aggressive\" fermentation behavior. A steady feeding with less starter can slowly increase in acidity without disturbing the balance much. Problematic patterns may also root from environmental factors, such as seasonal temperature changes affecting enzyme activity in the dough. Cultivating a consistent feeding routine and consistent control over environmental factors can lead to a more stable fermentation profile. It may also help to perform taste tests and monitor the starter's viscosity as quick, effective indicators of fermentation health, allowing you to adjust the feeding routine proactively.",
      "source": "target",
